	{
		"Glossary A-G",
		"Blox Cards has a lot of different words used to describe various concepts. Here is a list of all Blox Cards Jargon. You don't need to know most of this to play the game.\n\nA\n\\BAction: Cards that do their effect, then go into their controller's Graveyard. Actions have a bomb symbol on the bottom-right of their card. \n\n\\BAlternate Cost: Some cards allow you to pay different costs for the card under certain conditions. This doesn't change the card's total stud cost, only what you have to pay.\n\n\\BAnimate: Turn the terrain card in play into a fighter, and summon it under your control.\n\n\\BAOE: Meaning 'Area of Effect'. (See Splash)\n\n\\BArchetype: An older term for grouped cards. (See Group) \n\n\\BAssign:  Some cards deal damage in combat based on an effect, rather than their power. These are syntaxed \"This card assigns combat damage equal to...\"\n\nB\n\n\\BBan: When a player is banned, that player instantly loses the game. Used on alternate win conditions.\n\n\\BBaseplate: One of the six Zones of the game. During your end step, all cards in your Baseplate generate studs for you. The first card you discard each turn goes into your Baseplate. \n\n\\BBlessings: One-use abilities overlaid onto cards in your hand, triggered when that card is played. Blessings don't move around as you discard cards, allowing you to line up the blessing with a card by discarding. Most blessings are Eldritch Blessings, which gives a fighter cast from it 150 Power.\n\n\\BBoardwipe: A term referring to a Card that removes many Fighters at once. These are often used as a trump card from a losing situation.\n\n\\BBread: Slang for Targeted Effect Damage. Area of Effect Damage is referred to as AoE Bread.\n\n\\BBurn: Direct damage to your opponent from effects. A specialty of Red cards. Syntaxed \"Your Opponent loses [number] life.\"\n\nC \n\\BCard: A resource that you draw every turn. You can have 40 in your deck, and start the game with 7 of them.\n\n\\BCast: Playing a card from your hand.   \n\n\\BClone: Summon a copy of a given fighter. The clone has the base stats of that fighter.\n\n\\BControl: The player who has ownership of a given card.\n\n\\BColour: There are four colours in the game: Red, Blue, Green, and Yellow. Coloured studs go away after your turn ends. (See end step)\n\n\\BColourless: Colloquially called 'White' in the game. Cards can be colourless, and colourless studs do not go away after your turn ends. (See end step)\n\n\\BCombat: What happens when a fighter attacks another. The attacking fighter deals damage equal to its power, and the attacked fighter counterattacks with damage equal to its power.\n\n\\BCost: The amount of studs needed to cast a card.\n\n\\BCounter: A fighter deals damage to a fighter that attacks it. This is called countering. Some cards can't be countered, and some cards can't counter.\n\n\\BCrowd's Favour: A mechanic from the Pits of The Umberlords. If a player ever has a full board of eight fighters, they gain the 'Crowd's Favour' for the rest of the game. Some effects care about the Crowd's Favour, and it also gives the player an additional colourless stud each turn from their baseplate.\n\nD \n\\BDamage: A fighter loses health equal to the number specified. Normally from effect damage or by another fighter's power.\n\n\\BDeck: A personalised collection of 40 cards used to play the game. Change your current deck in the Deck Builder.\n\n\\BDestroy: Remove a Card from play and put it into its owner's Graveyard. This Triggers Ondeath Effects of Cards destroyed while on the Field. (See OnDeath)\n\n\\BDestroyImmune: Cards Immune to Destroy and HandDestroy Effects in all Zones. (See HandDestroy)\n\n\\BDiscard: Right-click a card, or tap & hold a card on mobile, to remove it from your hand and gain a stud of its colour. The first card discarded each turn goes into your Baseplate where it helps you cast stronger cards later on.\n\n\\BDivide: A mechanic in Solar Conquest. To divide a card X times, create X copies of it, with the original card's health and power split evenly between them, then obliterate the original. The original fighter needs more than 50 times X health for a division to go through.\n\n\\BDragonforce: Dragonforce is the engine of all Neodragons! This allows them to cast powerful actions and powerful fighters to the field.\n\n\\BDraw: Take a card from your deck and put it into your hand.\n\nE \n\\BEffect: The card's ability. Almost every card has an effect, so be sure to read the cards! Effects tell you how they trigger, if there's any additional requirements for the trigger, then read what the effect does!\n\n\\BEnd Step: The phase that happens when a player ends their turn. Their fighters Ready, Generate, Trigger Effects that trigger during end steps, and static effects cease.  \n\nF \n\\BFace: When you have no fighters, your face appears and your opponent can strike you with their fighters. Face damage is how you lose. Once your Lifepoints go to 0 from your Face being damaged, you lose the game! \n\n\\BFading: OnDeath effects aren't triggered when a Faded Fighter Dies. Fading Actions do not go to the Graveyard OnCast. With Fleeting: Fading will persist if the Card is returned to your Hand. (See Fleeting)\n\n\\BFatigue: If you would draw a card when you have no cards in your deck, you lose 100 Life for every additive draw failed this way. This is called fatigue.\n\n\\BField: One of the six Zones of the game. Players' fighters go here to do combat.\n\n\\BFighter: Cards with Health and Power that go onto the field when played.\n\n\\BFleeting: A Fleeting Card obliterates itself when its owner passes the End Step. Fleeting Cards automatically Discard from your Hand at your End Step and Generates in the next turn. Effects can trigger cards in the owner's Hand as Fleeting.\n\nG \n\\BGenerate: To gain a Stud of a Card's Colour. Fighters you control Generate during your End Step, as do cards in your Baseplate. \n\n\\BGive: To create a Card temporarily for that round and put it into its owner's Hand.\n\n\\BGraveyard: One of the six Zones of the game. Whenever a Fighter dies, it goes into its owner's Graveyard. All cards you discard except the first one each turn also go into your Graveyard.\n\n\\BGroup: A label (Doge, Infurnished, Shovelware, etc.) applied to similar cards to Group them together. To view a Card's Group, hover over it and check underneath its name.\n\n"
	},
	{
		"Glossary H-T",
		"Blox Cards has a lot of different words used to describe various concepts. Here is a list of all Blox Cards Jargon. You don't need to know most of this to play the game.\n\nH \n\\BHaste: Fighters that can attack the turn they're cast. \n\n\\BHealth: How much damage a Fighter can take before it dies.\n\n\\BHand: One of the six Zones of the game. A Player's Hand contains cards that can be Cast. A Player draws a Card into their Hand at the start of each turn. \n\n\\BHandDestroy: An effect that Discards Cards from your Hand and moves them to your Graveyard.\n\nI\n\\BInert: Inert Fighters can't Generate Studs in given Zones. Cards that are Inert when discarded don't go to the Baseplate when discarded either.\n\n\\BImmunities: Some cards are Immune to specific card effects, and ignore any instance of that Effect. For instance, cards immune to destruction card effects ignore any effect that says 'destroy.'\n\nL \n\\BLock: Locked Fighters cannot attack for a specific amount of turns. During your End Step, you remove a Lock from all Fighters. Most Fighters enter play locked for one turn, and many Blue Fighters can Lock other Fighters.\n\nM\n\\BMark: A Marked Fighter takes 100 extra Damage from all sources.\n\n\\BMill: Whenever a card is added to a full Hand in any way, such as Draw, Return, or Give, then the card is sent to the graveyard.\n\nO\n\\BObliterate: Completely remove a Card and not sending it into any Zone. This does not Trigger OnDeath Effects. Ignores Immunity Effects. (See OnDeath)\n\n\\BOverload: Excess Damage dealt by a Card with Overload is dealt to your Opponent's Life. For example, a 100 Health and 1000 Power Fighter with Overload attacking into a 600 Health and 500 Power Fighter will cause your Opponent to lose 400 Life.\n\nP \n\n\\BPower: How much Damage a Fighter deals when it fights in Combat.\n\nR\n\\BRage: A mechanic from Lunar Impact. If you control a Rageborne-grouped fighter and you discard your third card, you enter Rage. Entering Rage places a Rage stack into your Baseplate. During your turn, fighters you control deal 100 extra damage in combat for each stack of Rage you have. If you end the turn while in Rage, if you control an unlocked fighter, destroy it and all stacks of Rage you have.\n\n\\BReady: Remove exactly one Lock from a Fighter. Fighters Ready during their Owners' End Steps.\n\n\\BRecur: Put a Card from your Graveyard into your Hand.\n\n\\BRequirement: Some cards have conditions to be Cast. These are syntaxed \"Cast only if...\"\n\n\\BReset: Turn a fighter's Stats into its original Stats. This Effect is a variant of the Set Effect. (See Set)\n\n\\BReturn: Returning a card to its owner's Hand, forcing them to play it again. Returning while your Opponent has a full Hand causes the cards to be Milled. Every effect will stay on a card when its returned to the owner's Hand.\n\nS \n\\BSearch: To find a Card from a given Zone, usually the Deck.\n\n\\BSet: Makes a Fighter's Health and/or Power the given value.\n\n\\BShuffle: Give a Card and put it into a random position in your Deck. It can be drawn later.\n\n\\BSkip: Some Effects, normally on Terrains, can nullify other Effects. These last until your next End Step.\n\n\\BSplashing: Running a small amount of off-colour cards, cards that aren't normally seen in Decks that primarily use one specific Colour.\n\n\\BStaple: A term referring to a Card that is ideal for specific Decks.\n\n\\BStarter: A Coloured Fighter that costs exactly one, two, three, or four Colourless Studs and nothing else. Starters can also be any Card that has the 🌱 symbol in the Deck Builder.  Starters are more likely to appear in your opening Hand. Most decks should have around 6 starters.\n\n\\BStats: Describes the Health and Power of a Fighter.\n\n\\BSteal: Gain Control of a Target Fighter.\n\n\\BStealth: Effects don't Trigger when a Card with Stealth enters the Field.\n\n\\BStuds: The resource needed to Cast cards.\n\n\\BSummon: Putting a Card into the Field via an Effect. Summoned Fighters don't trigger OnCast Effects. (See OnCast)\n\n\\BSuspend: Removes a Fighter from the Field. At the beginning of the next End Step, it comes back into the Field unlocked under who it was Suspended from.\n\n\\BSwap: Switching the Health or Power of a Target Fighter.\n\nT \n\\BTarget: Certain effects ask you to choose a Fighter in the Field to be subject to its Effect. \n\n\\BTerrain: A Card type that triggers its Effect at the start of each turn. Terrains can only be removed by playing another Terrain, animating the Terrain, or specific Effects that Destroy Terrains.\n\n\\BTerrain Zone: One of the six Zones of the game. Terrain Zone houses the game's Terrain.\n\n\\BTerrified: A Terrified Card Obliterates itself during End Steps. (See Obliterate)\n\n\\BToken: A 'sixth' Rarity that apppears on certain cards which can only be given, summonend, or shuffled from effects. \n\n\\BTotal Stats: Checks Cards' total Health and Power combined in the Owners' Field.\n\n\\BTotal Stud Cost: The total number of Studs a Fighter needs to be Cast. A Card that costs CCCRRRGGG has a Total Stud Cost of 9.\n\n\\BTransform: Transforming always preserves health, power, and locks.\n\n\\BTrigger: The specific condition for a Card's Effect to happen. Triggers precede colons in a card effect and sometimes have an Effect Requirement. For example: \"When this Card is Cast, if you have 6500 or more Life: [Effect].\" Actions automatically Trigger when Cast.\n\n"
	},
